WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.460
YAIR WEINBERGER:
So I think by now

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.250
I don't need to convince
anyone that moving to the cloud

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.900
is useful and important.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.620
And I think a challenge
at many, many enterprises

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.120
face today is how to do it.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.760
And usually we have
this huge infrastructure

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.460
and servers and thousands
of different applications.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.980
And in order to understand
how to move them,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.210
we need to identify what
we want to move first, what

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.300
is going to be the most useful,
what is going to minimize

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.970
our disruption to our
current application, and most

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.850
importantly, how can we move
there as fast as possible.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.580
So in general, we talk
about three different steps

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.180
in migration.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.940
One is assessment,
understanding what

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.110
are the difficulties
that we are going to face

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.610
when we are going to migrate.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.440
What is the expected downtime?

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.630
What should we
communicate to our users?

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.130
Then we do the actual migration.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:06.120
After we finish migrating,
we tune our applications

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.550
to fit better to the cloud,
to fit better to the profile

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:12.480
that we are now running on GCP.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.390
So usually what we--
in the first step

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.910
we even decide what
apps we need to move

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.610
and what apps can we even
move, because sometimes

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.670
not everything can be
just lifted and shifted,

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.730
and we are going to talk
about this in a minute.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.040
But what dependencies
do we have?

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.050
Is everything going
to be shifted as is,

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.350
or are we going to
modernize something?

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.700
What is important
for application?

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.610
Do we have some specific
performance requirements

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:44.250
that might not be met
when we're in the cloud?

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.890
Do we have some specific
latency requirements?

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:51.390
And then, essentially,
that step will tell us what

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.100
is the right path to the cloud.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.110
And we have--

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.330
I think in terms
of tooling, this

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.670
is probably the part that
is the most difficult today.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.630
This is the part where there
is the least amount of tooling.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.120
We are going to show
one partner that

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.370
has built an assessment
tool for migrating

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.200
a specific type of application.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.930
But I think that,
in general, this

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.780
is the place where
we rely on knowledge,

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.260
and we rely on either the
engineers inside the company

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.300
or on our own PSO organization
to understand what the impact

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.850
is going to be for migration.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.760
And in general, we have
different migration types,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.340
and we need to
understand how exactly we

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.240
are going to migrate.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.970
I think probably the
best way to migrate

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.820
is the first one, to
retire the application.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.320
I mean, if we don't
need it anymore,

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.900
let's just shut it
down and that's it.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.230
Migration is done.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:47.070
Unfortunately, most
applications, we

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.500
don't follow this
pattern, and we actually

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.300
need to be more complex.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.000
I guess if we can
also replace it

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.013
with some new
vendor or something

00:02:56.013 --> 00:02:57.930
already in the cloud,
some SaaS vendor, that's

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.380
also pretty easy.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.740
We first move to that
vendor and then retire.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.100
But then when we
actually need to move it,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.520
then we have a lot of options.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:11.190
I guess the least
disruptive option, but also

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.550
usually the least useful,
is just to re-host it.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:19.110
We take our servers as is
and we migrate those servers

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.940
into the cloud.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.460
About six months ago
Google acquired Velostrata,

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.710
which is a company
that does VM migration,

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:31.320
and there are other Google
partners that do that as well.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:35.130
Then we start getting
into the realm of actually

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.750
changing something
in the application

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.180
when moving to the cloud.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.310
And again, this is usually
more disruptive, but also more

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.660
useful.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.760
So we can revise
our application.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.280
So maybe just move the VMs,
but also maybe containerize

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.640
some of them to be able to run
them on some container engine.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.580
So just a little bit of change.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.430
Or we can actually refactor,
so change the application

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.570
to different modules.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.970
Some modules actually
move to new stuff.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.040
Let's say we take
our database, which

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.470
we're going to talk about
in length in this talk,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.450
and move it to
Cloud SQL, and then

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.580
we take the other part
of our application

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.940
and just move the VM as is.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:18.060
And then we're going to the
things that usually are harder

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.310
to do, which is a complete
rebuild or complete rewrite

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.890
of the application
to fit the cloud.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:27.570
I think where we are going
to focus in this talk is

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.970
on the refactor
area, where we want

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.250
to take some of our
footprint and move it

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:36.120
into cloud-native tools,
mostly to databases.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.850
And then some of our footprint
we'll probably just keep as is,

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:43.605
and we'll just talk in
the same API the same way

00:04:43.605 --> 00:04:45.480
but to new databases
that exist in the cloud.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.340
And then once we're
done, of course,

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:51.570
we are going to optimize.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.580
We're going to try
to optimize for cost,

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:57.510
going to optimize for speed,
going to optimize for latency.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:00.960
That's only once
we're done moving.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.420
And with GCP we
can actually-- we

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:08.160
have built and spent a
lot of time and effort

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.690
in order to build
migrations better.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.780
And one is of
course fundamentally

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:16.470
making the cloud itself better,
making it scale, the network

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:21.000
performance, and the ability
to actually transfer data

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.800
at great speed, the service
level on the SLOs of GCP.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.420
And then the migration
capabilities itself,

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:32.730
which are migration features,
migration tools, and migration

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:33.780
partners.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:36.870
And eventually you're
not alone in this.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.050
We have a very good
PSO organization

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.477
and support that will help
you throughout the process.

00:05:43.477 --> 00:05:45.810
And as I mentioned, usually
during the assessment phase,

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:49.860
the pre-migration step is where
the organization is currently

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:52.740
most important compared to
the tooling that exists today,

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:59.220
but of course it's also very
useful in the migration itself,

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.220
and then in the tuning, because
those are people that actually

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:05.400
know what's best and
what best tool to use

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.610
and how to use
them in the cloud.

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:11.820
So let's talk about
different migration types.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.150
One thing that actually works
very well today in Google Cloud

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:20.220
is we have the cloud compatible
of the on-premise stuff for--

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:24.450
Cloud SQL works both
for MySQL and Postgres.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:25.920
We have Memorystore
for Redis which

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:28.080
is an API compatible to Redis.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.210
And Bigtable and HBase
are also API compatible.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.690
So if those are the dependencies
of your application,

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:35.970
refactoring should
be pretty easy,

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.400
as you will not need
to change any APIs,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.460
you will not need to
change any drivers,

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:42.600
and your application
should just work

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.630
as is exactly the
same way it works

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:47.370
against the
on-premise databases.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:50.000
So usually a very
common migration pass,

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.580
and the one that we are
going to extensively cover

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:56.750
in this session, is to move the
databases themselves to Cloud

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:00.900
SQL or Memorystore, and then
move the application using

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.920
probably VM migration.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:04.680
And then you don't
need to change

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.425
anything in the application
itself or in the drivers.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:14.100
And then, I would say maybe
the more interesting, but also

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.990
not as easy migration
path is when

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:18.050
we want to modernize database.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:24.700
And this happens usually for
one of maybe two reasons.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.880
One reason is we
need more scale.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.200
We need better-- our MySQL
instance can no longer fit

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.590
the bill of what we need, or our
Postgres instance is no longer

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.090
scalable enough
for our data needs.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:39.660
And that's why
we've built Spanner.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:43.830
And Spanner is a low latency,
very high availability,

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:47.160
and scalable database
system in the cloud.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.190
And it's not API
compatible in MySQL,

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:51.900
not API compatible
with Postgres,

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.492
but very, very similar.

00:07:53.492 --> 00:07:55.200
So usually most of
the things that you're

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.250
able to do with MySQL and
Postgres, most of your queries

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.380
will just shift as is.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.770
There will be some
work on understanding

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:08.490
what's not exactly compatible
and then moving that.

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:13.290
The other reason is I
have tools that are--

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.900
I would say I'm using
them for many years now

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.460
and I want to modernize them.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.490
So I'm using Oracle, for
example, I'm using Cassandra,

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:25.590
and I want to move either to
Cloud SQL, to Cloud Bigtable.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.320
Usually the main reason
we see moving Oracle

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:31.770
to Postgres in the cloud
is to reduce costs.

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:41.429
And then the Cassandra migration
that we see is just sometimes

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:44.460
at a certain size, it is
becoming very difficult

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.710
to manage your own
Cassandra cluster,

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:50.500
to understand the production
issues that are happening.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:51.960
And then moving to
Bigtable usually

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.660
takes away a lot of this load.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:57.720
We do have, for this--
again, the tooling here

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:02.550
are not as advanced, but we
do have a lot of guidelines,

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:05.220
a lot of experience, a lot
of migration guidelines that

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:06.930
will help you do this
and take you step

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:09.870
by step throughout
that process of doing

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:14.150
the heterogeneous
database migration.

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:16.152
So as some of you
may have heard,

00:09:16.152 --> 00:09:17.360
Alooma acquired Google Cloud.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:19.610
This actually has become
official last week.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:22.400
Alooma has been focused
on data migration

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.700
from the beginning
five years ago,

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.760
and inside Google, myself
and the entire Alooma team

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:31.640
is going to be focused on
delivering a great database

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:33.890
migration experience,
and then expanding

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:36.500
the current capabilities
of the database migrations.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.600
And the other side
is we don't think

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:45.625
that we can provide
everything ourselves,

00:09:45.625 --> 00:09:48.000
and we don't think that you
can do everything yourselves.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.960
And that's why we've
partnered with great companies

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:54.460
like Striim, and the other tool
that I'm going to show today

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:58.330
is a migVisor by NAYA Tech
which helps with that process

00:09:58.330 --> 00:09:59.180
as well.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:03.760
So with the partner ecosystem
and the in-house tooling

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:08.140
that we've built and
your own engineers,

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:13.754
the goal is to make that
migration as easy as possible.

00:10:13.754 --> 00:10:15.280
We have some customer examples.

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:19.100
So one example is Evernote.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:22.570
Evernote have moved over
five billion user notes

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:25.995
into GCP in two months, a
little bit over two months.

00:10:25.995 --> 00:10:28.290
Pantheon migrated
200,000 websites

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:32.380
which they host from on-prem
to MySQL, just moving the MySQL

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:35.980
instance from on-prem to
GCP, in a couple of weeks.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:40.470
And then Optiva moved
from Oracle to Spanner,

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:43.010
saw about 10x increase
in database performance.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:47.920
So doing those migrations
are actually useful.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:51.170
And usually you
probably ask yourself,

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:54.760
why would I enter
that migration,

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:56.630
that painful migration project?

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:59.340
It might take me a long time,
it might take me a long effort.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:00.890
Usually is it worth it.

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:04.170
Sometimes it's not as painful
as you think it's going to be.

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:06.940
Sometimes the existing tools
can actually make it easier,

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:09.470
and you can get it done in
two weeks or in a month.

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:12.580
And sometimes just the
performance impact,

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:14.740
the cost impact, is
so great that it's

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:18.590
just worth it again as well.

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:22.780
So with that, let's try and
focus a little bit on migration

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:25.690
into Cloud SQL and
how to lift and shift

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:28.870
your application, hopefully
with minimal downtime

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:29.920
and minimal effort.

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:35.380
In general, the
recommendation is of course,

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:38.860
whenever possible, try and
take the simple approach.

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:42.460
And the simple approach is
just a downtime migration.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:46.360
So let's say I'm doing a like to
like, a homogeneous migration.

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:49.090
I have a MySQL instance on-prem.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:52.630
I want to move it to the
same instance in the cloud.

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:55.557
Usually I would
tell my users, hey,

00:11:55.557 --> 00:11:56.890
there is going to be a downtime.

00:11:56.890 --> 00:12:00.010
That downtime, of course,
depends on the database size.

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:03.070
Stop writing to the
source database,

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:06.250
perform a dump,
like a MySQL dump

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:09.940
or whatever to move the
schema and the data itself,

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:13.180
and in the MySQL dump itself,
we can also move triggers.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:15.890
We can move anything
that we need.

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:18.100
Spin up a new
instance in the cloud.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:21.760
Load the data to the
instance in the cloud.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:26.230
Switch the DNS or the direction
to that server, and that's it,

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:27.490
we're done.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:28.780
We would re-enable writes.

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:31.830
We re-enable all of the clients
throughout your database,

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:33.310
and that's very simple.

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:39.160
Unfortunately it's not always
possible to take downtime.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:42.010
And that can be from
one of two reasons.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:45.950
One reason can be the
database is just too big,

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:52.490
so the downtime
will be too long.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:54.970
The other is that it's
business-critical application.

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:57.670
We won't be able
to take downtime.

00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:01.090
One way, again, is to try
and make some optimizations

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:02.860
to reduce that downtime.

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:04.930
And actually we
have an [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:09.400
These are some real life great
tips to reduce the downtime

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:11.090
when doing the migrations.

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:15.800
So one is when importing the
data, disable all foreign keys,

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:19.330
all secondary keys, because
that way the database will not

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:20.380
validate against them.

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:24.070
So there is no need to
actually do that validation,

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:27.430
because you already know you're
moving the entire data set.

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:29.050
And then when you
import data, also

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:31.450
import it and dump it
of course in the order

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:32.800
of the primary key.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:36.640
That usually makes import-- that
can have very, very significant

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:40.090
impact on the import time.

00:13:40.090 --> 00:13:43.450
In terms of what are we
choosing to import into,

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:46.060
we want to make
sure that if we can

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:49.390
have the available RAM
larger than the data set,

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:52.480
it means that the import
time will be much faster.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:57.910
Using SD storage or
high IOPS also greatly

00:13:57.910 --> 00:14:00.993
reduces downtime, increases
migration performance.

00:14:00.993 --> 00:14:02.410
And then during
the import process

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:04.240
itself, of course,
we are looking

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:09.640
at how fast the storage grows,
do we have any read operation,

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:11.800
do we have paging
that's happening,

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:15.790
and understand why import
is taking some time,

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:17.495
and also how to reduce it.

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:21.500
So these are all fine.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.280
These are great and
great tips, but still not

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:26.170
good enough oftentimes.

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:29.920
Like, oftentimes, still, we
cannot take the downtime,

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:31.630
or the downtime will
still be too long.

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:35.710
And usually if we have a one
terabyte or several terabytes

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:39.343
MySQL instance, you just
cannot take the downtime,

00:14:39.343 --> 00:14:41.260
or if you have a
business-critical application

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:42.820
that keeps writing, you
need to keep writing,

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:44.195
you cannot take
downtime as well.

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:51.090
I'll go back to what
we can do after import,

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:58.960
but that's why we have an option
of minimizing the downtime

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:00.010
when migrating.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.650
So this is essentially
zero downtime,

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:06.910
and this is already available
for MySQL to MySQL migration.

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:09.578
In your GCP instance
you can just click.

00:15:09.578 --> 00:15:11.620
I'm not sure if you've
ever seen this button that

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:14.650
says migrate data,
but this will actually

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:19.390
do the migration with very
low downtime or zero downtime.

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:23.590
And the way it's
done is it will--

00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:25.150
we have our application.

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:28.150
It's still reading against
the existing database.

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:31.800
What that process is doing
is that process starts a new

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:36.640
instance, starts replication, so
like binlog replication running

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:41.650
between your source on-prem
and the database in the cloud

00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:44.470
as if it was another
of the slaves of--

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:45.880
as if it was a database slave.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:52.740
At some point, the data between
the source and the target

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:55.710
matches, and then
we can pause writes,

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:58.440
and then we can really have
minimal downtime, maybe

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:00.510
for a minute or
for a few minutes,

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:02.740
pause writes to the
existing instance,

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:05.510
move it to the new
instance, and then resume.

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:07.800
And because we had
replication running,

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:09.490
the data will be
exactly the same.

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:12.690
So we can actually move
with minimal downtime.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.210
And from our
experience, what we've

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:18.300
seen with that minimal downtime
migration are sometimes

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:21.140
the make or break of
will I ever migrate

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:23.640
my application to the cloud,
will I ever migrate my database

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.290
to the cloud.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:28.050
So for the MySQL
to MySQL use case,

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:31.490
that option is
already available.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:34.770
We'll go in and we'll
show it real quick.

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:44.350
So let's say I
have a data source.

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:47.770
I'm pointing myself to the IP
address, username, password

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:49.600
of the source database.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:52.472
I'm choosing the instance type
of what I'm going to migrate.

00:16:52.472 --> 00:16:54.430
And again, best to choose
according to the tape

00:16:54.430 --> 00:16:56.500
that we've shown earlier.

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:58.080
And I'm pointing
it to a dump file.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:03.640
So let's say I ran MySQL dump
at some point in the near past

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:06.609
and I put it in a GCS.

00:17:06.609 --> 00:17:09.079
I'm pointing it to a dump file.

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:11.599
I'm creating a replica.

00:17:11.599 --> 00:17:15.160
And after a few minutes,
that replica will be created

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:16.970
and will have its own IP.

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:18.640
And once the replica
is created, we

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:21.329
can actually start
our application.

00:17:21.329 --> 00:17:23.079
All you need to do in
your source database

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:24.730
is give permission.

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:28.150
Once you've given
permission to the database

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:30.010
to connect to a
slave, you'll see

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:32.500
the replica starts creating.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:36.970
So in that case it's a read
replica that's being created,

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:42.010
and you can start monitoring
the CPU, the utilization,

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:43.450
the storage, and
you can actually

00:17:43.450 --> 00:17:45.190
see this is a fast forward.

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:48.310
It was a very big database, took
about 30 minutes to migrate.

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:50.050
This is a fast forward
for two minutes,

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:51.770
but you see the
storage climbing up,

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.770
and then the latency
is being measured.

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:57.400
The latency
essentially tells you

00:17:57.400 --> 00:18:00.640
what's the difference
between my current replica

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:03.760
and the original server in
terms of how far am I behind.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:06.260
So at the beginning you'll see
it growing, growing, growing,

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:06.760
growing.

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:09.490
And at that point
it was 30 minutes.

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:13.660
At some point that
replication is done,

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:16.930
and we are catching
up on the binlog,

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:18.460
catching up on the binlog.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:23.540
Once we've read all of the
binlogs from the source,

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:26.560
we would actually see the
latency going down to zero.

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:28.020
At that point, you can click--

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:31.240
you know what, what you've
seen there was promote replica.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:35.620
Once we promote replica, we stop
write to the source database.

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:37.000
We're not writing anymore.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:38.250
We click Promote Replica.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:41.180
As you've seen, that process
takes about 30 seconds,

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:42.970
which means it's no
longer replicating.

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:45.220
It's now the primary server.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:49.240
And once it's a primary
server and it is promoted,

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:54.700
we can actually start the
writes from the application

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:56.727
directly to that replica.

00:18:56.727 --> 00:18:57.310
And that's it.

00:18:57.310 --> 00:18:58.270
We're done.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:18:59.920
We had a minimal
downtime migration

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:01.880
between MySQL and MySQL.

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:04.240
If we can go back to the deck--

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:21.800
Great.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:24.860
So after the import itself--

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:27.500
I'm backing up a
little bit here--

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:29.990
essentially, once we import
it, once our application starts

00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:31.700
writing, we start to tune it.

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:33.770
We are monitoring
the performance.

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:35.657
We see do we have
too many writes,

00:19:35.657 --> 00:19:36.740
do we have too many reads.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:40.700
Once the application's actually
running, we can [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:41.870
to improve the performance.

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:45.020
We can maybe change the instance
type if we need more CPU

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:46.880
or if we need more memory.

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:48.550
We can turn on
binary logging if you

00:19:48.550 --> 00:19:50.720
want to create our own replica.

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.130
We're, again, having MySQL
instances in the cloud,

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:54.547
we can have a
replica, we can have

00:19:54.547 --> 00:19:58.040
a read replica, can turn on
binary logging on our Cloud SQL

00:19:58.040 --> 00:19:59.300
instance.

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:01.200
And essentially
we're ready to go.

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:04.580
We have our application running
against database in the cloud.

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:07.670
At this point we can keep
running our application VMs

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:08.570
on-prem.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:11.240
We can take the application VMs
and migrate them into the cloud

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:16.590
as well to have the entire
application in the cloud.

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:21.710
So this was for a homogeneous
MySQL to MySQL migration.

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:23.720
That's great.

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:27.810
What happens if we want to
do a more complex migration?

00:20:27.810 --> 00:20:32.070
Let's say we want to
migrate Oracle to Postgres.

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:34.722
So we have a big
Oracle instance on-prem

00:20:34.722 --> 00:20:36.430
and we want to modernize
our application.

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:40.400
We want to move to
Postgres in the cloud.

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:43.070
So here, actually,
assessment is very important.

00:20:43.070 --> 00:20:48.620
And this tool is tool by
NAYA Tech called migVisor,

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:51.860
and it provides you
with information.

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:56.270
How difficult will it be to
migrate your Oracle instance

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:57.028
into Postgres?

00:20:57.028 --> 00:20:59.320
So you will choose a source,
you would choose a target.

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:02.490
In that case I've chosen
Oracle to Postgres,

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:04.460
and I have here five
or six databases.

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:06.920
What's nice about
this tool, all it does

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:09.590
is it will query
the Oracle metadata.

00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:11.620
It will just run a bunch of--

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:14.680
a couple of thousand queries
on the Oracle metadata,

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:17.540
understand the current
usage, understand

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:21.950
are you using very
specific Oracle synonyms,

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:24.808
for example, that doesn't
exist on Cloud SQL.

00:21:24.808 --> 00:21:26.100
What do we need to do about it?

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:29.330
Are you using
PL/SQL extensively,

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:31.470
and then you would need
to convert that PL/SQL.

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:33.560
Eventually one of
your databases we'll

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:36.530
get a grade, a
score, of how complex

00:21:36.530 --> 00:21:39.980
will it be to migrate that
database, which is that bar

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:41.580
chart that you can see here.

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:44.060
So the ones with
the higher score

00:21:44.060 --> 00:21:45.790
are more complex migrations.

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:48.980
The ones with the
lower score should just

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:51.200
migrate very easily,
because you're not

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:55.670
using any specific or
proprietary Oracle stuff.

00:21:55.670 --> 00:21:57.620
And you can actually
even see why.

00:21:57.620 --> 00:22:00.480
So I guess resolution
here is not good enough,

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:02.790
but you can actually
dive deeper and say,

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:04.400
hey, this is very complex.

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:06.230
Why is it so complex?

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:08.520
And then you will see
these are the features,

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:12.570
the specific features, that
are being used by the--

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:15.020
which are not
compatible with Postgres

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:17.630
or will need some
manual intervention

00:22:17.630 --> 00:22:19.320
in order to move to Postgres.

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:22.120
And you can even see which
users are using this feature.

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:25.320
So you can say, hey, my CRM
app, maybe I'm going to keep it

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:28.700
in Oracle for now because it's
using some very custom stuff

00:22:28.700 --> 00:22:29.510
that I cannot move.

00:22:29.510 --> 00:22:34.940
But my billing app I might
be able to move to Postgres.

00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:36.440
And then the last
thing that it will

00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:43.010
do, it will also analyze
if the Oracle usage is more

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:48.980
of analytics or OLAP
usage type, or is

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:51.410
it OLTP transactional type.

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:54.230
And if it's
analytics usage type,

00:22:54.230 --> 00:22:56.840
you might be better
served with migrating

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:59.690
that specific application
or that specific usage

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:01.400
into BigQuery and
not into Postgres.

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:04.610
So it will tell you, hey,
this type of application

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:06.440
is using mostly OLAP queries.

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:08.820
Consider not moving to
Postgres, but moving

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:11.270
to a data warehouse
that's designed

00:23:11.270 --> 00:23:13.130
for analytics like BigQuery.

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:19.040
And those applications are
doing mostly OLTP, or mostly

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:25.250
transactional load, and then
it's better to go to Postgres.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:27.800
So currently NAYA
Tech and migVisor

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:30.920
supports the assessment
of migrations from Oracle

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:34.700
to Cloud SQL, Postgres and
MySQL, and coming soon by them

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:37.430
is also migrations into Spanner.

00:23:37.430 --> 00:23:42.740
And with that, I want to
call on Codin and Alok

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:45.380
to show us a demo of the
actual migration with Striim.

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:47.330
So we've finished
the assessment.

00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:50.250
We decided that we want
to do the migration.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:52.670
We want to move over
our Oracle to Postgres.

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:54.110
One way of doing
it is to Striim.

