OK.

00:24:23.267 --> 00:24:25.350
Just want to make sure my
clicker is working here.

00:24:28.823 --> 00:24:30.240
Let's go to the
next slide, Codin.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:30.985
Sorry.

00:24:30.985 --> 00:24:34.300
All right, just a little bit
before I get into the demo.

00:24:34.300 --> 00:24:36.370
So Striim is a next
generation platform,

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:41.500
and we focus on three
different solution categories.

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:44.760
The categories are cloud
adoption, hybrid cloud data

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:47.910
integration, and in-memory
stream processing.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.100
Of course, the focus today
is on cloud adoption,

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:54.570
and I'm delighted to announce
a partnership with Google

00:24:54.570 --> 00:25:01.430
on cloud adoption, especially in
the area of database migration.

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:05.420
So what I'm going to focus
today is on the Oracle

00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:10.520
to Cloud SQL Postgres migration,
and especially as Yair pointed

00:25:10.520 --> 00:25:13.500
out, that downtime
is a big problem,

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:15.913
especially for
mission-critical applications.

00:25:15.913 --> 00:25:18.080
So you might have a number
of different applications

00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:20.360
from your CRM,
billing, payments,

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:22.753
core banking, et cetera.

00:25:22.753 --> 00:25:24.170
And these might
not necessarily be

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:26.582
able to take any
kind of an outage,

00:25:26.582 --> 00:25:28.040
but you still want
to actually move

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:32.030
onto your initiatives
for cloud modernization,

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:35.120
or for continuous operations.

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:36.980
You might also have
data on other clouds,

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:40.040
and you wanted to synchronize
that with newer applications

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:42.620
that you're deploying
on the Google Cloud.

00:25:42.620 --> 00:25:45.110
So those are all the
benefits that you gain.

00:25:45.110 --> 00:25:49.010
Of course, the critical problem
here is the downtime part.

00:25:49.010 --> 00:25:50.653
So let's get into
the demo, and I'm

00:25:50.653 --> 00:25:52.986
going to actually show you
