00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:05.160
And this is the landing
page of the Striim product.

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:06.510
There are three parts here.

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:07.910
There's a dashboard space.

00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:08.840
There's the apps.

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:09.930
Apps are the pipeline.

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:12.020
So you'll hear me
interchangeably

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:14.190
use apps and data pipelines.

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:15.998
And then there's also--

00:26:15.998 --> 00:26:18.540
you can actually preview your
sources before you get started.

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:21.890
So we're going to jump into
the apps part of the demo.

00:26:21.890 --> 00:26:25.820
What you see here are prebuilt
pipelines, and remember,

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:29.540
this is a zero downtime type
of scenario I'm talking about.

00:26:29.540 --> 00:26:31.330
This has two critical phases.

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:34.050
There's phase one,
which is instantiation.

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:37.340
That's where you make a one-time
copy of all of the data, which

00:26:37.340 --> 00:26:39.260
could be gigabytes,
perhaps terabytes,

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:42.530
or it could be lesser,
and then followed

00:26:42.530 --> 00:26:45.273
by a catch up or
synchronization phase, which

00:26:45.273 --> 00:26:46.940
we achieve through
some very specialized

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:50.240
readers that help you catch
up the source and the target.

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:53.010
And ultimately, the two sides
are going to be in sync.

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:57.390
So let's step into the
initialization phase.

00:26:57.390 --> 00:27:00.590
So this is a simple
pipeline that

00:27:00.590 --> 00:27:04.610
shows a pipeline going from
an on-premise Oracle database

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:07.370
into a Cloud SQL
Postgres database.

00:27:07.370 --> 00:27:09.890
The way this was built
was using a flow designer,

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:13.280
by choosing a number of
out of the box components

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:15.920
where you configure your
sources, for example,

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:17.480
and your targets.

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:19.618
And you can also transform
the data along the way,

00:27:19.618 --> 00:27:22.160
because it may be interesting,
because the data formats might

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:25.160
be different, or you may want
to re-align some of the data

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:27.410
as you're going through.

00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:28.910
Let's step into
the configuration

00:27:28.910 --> 00:27:30.595
of just the Oracle database.

00:27:30.595 --> 00:27:31.970
So here's where
you configure all

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:34.760
of the different
properties, and it's

00:27:34.760 --> 00:27:39.140
pretty flexible in terms of
how you want to move the data.

00:27:39.140 --> 00:27:41.750
On the cloud side, here's
where you provide your service

00:27:41.750 --> 00:27:46.610
account, your connections into
the Postgres SQL database.

00:27:46.610 --> 00:27:49.460
In this case, we are going
to be actually moving

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:51.950
two separate tables for
the purposes of the demo.

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:53.840
We're going to attempt
to do a live migration

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:56.840
of a million records of
the line items table,

00:27:56.840 --> 00:28:00.440
as well as do some DML
activity in phase two

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:03.420
with a change in the capture
for the orders table.

00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:05.760
So let's go ahead and
deploy the application.

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:09.650
And Striim can run in
the cloud or on-prem.

00:28:09.650 --> 00:28:10.320
It's up to you.

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:12.080
It's pretty flexible.

00:28:12.080 --> 00:28:17.810
And as we deploy it, you can
see that there's actually--

00:28:17.810 --> 00:28:20.060
there's a deliberate
mismatch that we introduced.

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:22.550
You heard Yair talk about
some of the assessment

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:23.637
checks and so forth.

00:28:23.637 --> 00:28:25.220
So it's important,
before you actually

00:28:25.220 --> 00:28:28.310
do the migration, that the
sources and the target,

00:28:28.310 --> 00:28:31.250
schemas, et cetera, sort of
add up, they're compatible,

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:32.970
there's no
exceptions, et cetera.

00:28:32.970 --> 00:28:35.750
So here it did detect that
there's one incompatible data

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:36.420
type.

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:39.590
So we'll go ahead and just
skip this specific one.

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:42.890
We have a-- Codin has actually
fixed this in another flow.

00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:45.083
So we're going to jump into
that initial load flow.

00:28:45.083 --> 00:28:46.500
So let's go ahead
and deploy that.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:50.840
And once we deploy it
it's ready to get started.

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:54.840
We want to check, Codin, whether
the tables are already empty.

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:57.000
So in this case in
the line item table,

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:58.747
you can see that
the count is zero.

00:28:58.747 --> 00:29:01.080
And now we're going to go
ahead and run the application.

00:29:01.080 --> 00:29:04.380
That's the one that actually
goes to your critical database,

00:29:04.380 --> 00:29:06.008
which is Oracle on-prem.

00:29:06.008 --> 00:29:07.550
Go ahead and you
can run that, Codin.

00:29:07.550 --> 00:29:09.750
Start the application.

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:13.825
We'll also preview the
data along the way.

00:29:13.825 --> 00:29:16.160
You can see now the data
is beginning to flow in.

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:19.540
In this case you have a million
records in the line items

00:29:19.540 --> 00:29:21.770
table, and that
sort of keeps going,

00:29:21.770 --> 00:29:24.720
and you can also monitor
the application progress.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:29.520
You can see that so far,
we have 400,000 imports.

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:31.860
The output and input
obviously are changing.

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:33.920
So this is where a lot of
the magic is happening.

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:36.320
So things like
batch optimization,

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:39.230
things like parallelism,
things like event guarantees,

00:29:39.230 --> 00:29:41.450
things like commit
semantics, all of this stuff

00:29:41.450 --> 00:29:43.070
is taking place
behind the scenes.

00:29:43.070 --> 00:29:46.370
So it will be transactionally
consistent when you actually

00:29:46.370 --> 00:29:47.567
complete the migration.

00:29:47.567 --> 00:29:48.650
So now I think we're done.

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:50.900
Why don't we take a
look at the Postgres SQL

00:29:50.900 --> 00:29:53.252
database, make sure that--

00:29:53.252 --> 00:29:53.960
and there you go.

00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:57.650
So your million records table
was just moved pretty quickly.

00:29:57.650 --> 00:29:59.570
And so the performance
is very impressive.

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:02.330
With that, what
I want to do is--

00:30:02.330 --> 00:30:05.220
remember, this could be
gigabytes or terabytes of data.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:07.730
What we want to do
is during this time,

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:10.790
all of the data that is actually
changing in the database--

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:13.620
so this is your
actual activity-- that

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:16.700
is being moved using a technique
called change data capture.

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:19.990
So Striim allows you to actually
have a specialized reader,

00:30:19.990 --> 00:30:23.330
and in this case
let's step into that.

00:30:23.330 --> 00:30:25.440
So here's where the
reader properties are.

00:30:25.440 --> 00:30:28.560
In the interest of time,
we can go a little faster.

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:31.890
Same configuration for
the target obviously.

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:33.680
And in this case,
now, what we do

00:30:33.680 --> 00:30:36.380
is we take activity from
the redo log of the Oracle

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:39.680
database, and we are going
to effectively replay that

00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:41.690
on the target side
to catch them up,

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:43.730
thereby avoiding
any kind of outage

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:45.330
to the production database.

00:30:45.330 --> 00:30:48.170
So again, in this case,
we have a mismatch.

00:30:48.170 --> 00:30:49.795
Here it's more of a
precision mismatch.

00:30:49.795 --> 00:30:51.712
In this case I'll ignore
it, because I already

00:30:51.712 --> 00:30:54.110
know that all my data values
adhere to the target site's

00:30:54.110 --> 00:30:54.750
schema.

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:56.208
So go ahead and
ignore that, and we

00:30:56.208 --> 00:30:58.500
want to actually go ahead
and move the table here.

00:30:58.500 --> 00:31:00.460
Again, the preview
option is selected,

00:31:00.460 --> 00:31:02.880
and let's go ahead and start it.

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:05.160
Codin's going to generate
some DML activity using

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:07.720
a separate simulator.

00:31:07.720 --> 00:31:10.180
This is where you're going
to see some inserts, updates,

00:31:10.180 --> 00:31:11.540
and deletes.

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:13.230
And you can see
that he's running

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:17.340
a number of DML operations
against the orders table.

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:19.370
And then Striim,
you can actually

00:31:19.370 --> 00:31:22.030
already see that now not only
are we capturing the data,

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:22.965
but also the metadata.

00:31:22.965 --> 00:31:24.840
So these are things from
the Oracle database,

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:26.970
like the transaction
ID, the system

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:28.620
commit number, all
of the metadata,

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:31.350
the table in, the operation
type, insert, update, delete,

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:33.300
you can filter on
stuff like this.

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:35.100
And then you can
actually finally

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:37.380
log into the orders
table and see

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:40.420
that there is, in fact,
data present in this table.

00:31:40.420 --> 00:31:42.090
This was empty beforehand.

00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:45.960
So this is sort of a combined
technique of instantiation

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:49.140
followed by change data capture
and application that allows you

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:52.680
to, at your own leisure,
move your on-prem database

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:53.730
into the cloud database.

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:55.800
You can actually go
ahead and test both sides

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:57.160
while the migration is ongoing.

00:31:57.160 --> 00:32:00.100
It may take you a day, a
week, or potentially months,

00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:01.930
depending on the
size of the database,

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:04.170
but the key benefits are
that there is no outage

00:32:04.170 --> 00:32:06.490
and you can keep your
operations continuous for your

00:32:06.490 --> 00:32:07.740
mission-critical applications.

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:11.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

